{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/care",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "care"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "The ethical and legal recognition of the relational responsibilities that support human dignity and autonomy, conceived not as dependence or paternalism, but as a rights‑based practice ensuring inclusion, assistance, and mutual respect within society."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "Feminist theories have been crucial in reshaping the concept of care, by highlighting it as a central social and ethical practice rather than private, gendered labour. They exposed how traditional views undervalued caregiving, redefined care as a source of moral knowledge and social cohesion, and promoted the idea that interdependence and relational responsibility are fundamental to justice and equality. The ethics of care highlight that vulnerability and interdependence are universal human conditions and that care should be organised socially and politically, not left to private charity or individual sacrifice. In this perspective, the CRPD and EU law can be read as institutional embodiments of the ethics of care. The CRPD reframes care as a means of empowerment and inclusion, emphasising personal assistance, community living, and the shared responsibility of states, families, and societies."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "1.3"
  ],
  "narrower": [
    {
      "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/caregiver",
